      <h2 id="sec3">HubSpot email marketing: best if you live in a CRM</h2>
      <p>HubSpot email marketing is the safest all-in-one choice when your contacts already sit in HubSpot's CRM. The free tools include email marketing with HubSpot branding and a cap of 2,000 sends a month, so HubSpot free email marketing is a real starting point. Marketing Hub Starter begins at roughly $9–$15 per seat monthly. Automation, A/B testing and reporting improve sharply on Professional, which costs far more.</p>
      <p>For outreach, the catch is sequences. One-to-one sales sequences need Sales Hub Professional, reported at around $100 per seat per month plus an onboarding fee, and HubSpot still expects consent for marketing sends.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p>Verdict: great for nurturing leads who already know you. Expensive and wrong-shaped for cold outreach.</p></div>

      <h2 id="sec4">Zoho Campaigns review: pricing, features and Zoho marketing automation</h2>
      <p>Zoho Campaigns is the cheapest credible option here. The free plan covers about 2,000 contacts and 6,000 emails a month, and Zoho Campaigns pricing for paid plans starts at only a few dollars monthly before scaling with contact count. It syncs both ways with Zoho CRM, so email behavior flows back to your sales team.</p>
      <p>If you outgrow it, Zoho Marketing Automation and Zoho Marketing Plus add journeys, lead scoring and multichannel campaigns. The trade-off is polish: the interface takes longer to learn than Mailchimp's, and it is permission-based, so it is not a cold email tool.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p>Verdict: the best-value pick for opted-in lists, especially if you already run Zoho CRM or Zoho One.</p></div>

      <h2 id="sec5">Mailchimp: the famous name with a shrinking free plan</h2>
      <p>Mailchimp is the most recognized platform, with a huge template library and 300+ integrations. But its free plan keeps shrinking: in February 2026 it dropped to 250 contacts and 500 sends a month, and unsubscribed contacts count toward your total.</p>
      <p>Zoho Campaigns vs Mailchimp is the common face-off. Zoho wins on price and CRM integration. Mailchimp wins on ease of use and templates. For cold outreach, Mailchimp is a hard no.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p>Verdict: fine for newsletters and ecommerce, wrong for prospecting.</p></div>

      <h2 id="sec6">Brevo email marketing reviews and MailerLite: cheaper Mailchimp alternatives</h2>
      <p>Brevo prices by emails sent rather than contacts stored. Its free plan allows 300 emails a day, paid plans start around $9–$10 a month, and transactional email is included, which suits ecommerce and SaaS. Brevo email marketing reviews tend to praise the value and fault the advanced reporting.</p>
      <p>MailerLite is the simple option: a clean editor, visual automation and low prices, billed by subscriber count. Its free tier is small, so check the current limit. Both are opt-in only.</p>

      <h2 id="sec7">Best cold email platforms: Instantly, Smartlead, lemlist and ManyReach</h2>
      <p>These four are what you actually want for cold outreach. Each connects your own Google Workspace or Microsoft mailboxes, warms them up and rotates sends.</p>
      <h3>Instantly</h3>
      <p>From about $37–$47 a month, with unlimited mailboxes and warm-up. It is the easiest to learn and the best fit for founders and small email-only teams.</p>
      <h3>Smartlead</h3>
      <p>From about $39 a month. White-label options, client separation and an API make it the agency favorite. The interface is denser, and you bring your own lead data.</p>
      <h3>lemlist</h3>
      <p>Its Email plan is reported at $55 a month yearly or $69 monthly per workspace, and multichannel with LinkedIn is priced per user. It leads on personalization, but costs more than Instantly or Smartlead for pure volume.</p>
      <h3>ManyReach</h3>
      <p>Pay-as-you-go credits instead of a subscription, listed around $99 for 10,000 credits, with unlimited mailbox connections, built-in warm-up and 250 free emails to test. Disclosure: we use ManyReach for Tycoon Clap's own outreach, so test the free credits yourself before trusting our view.</p>

      <h2 id="sec8">Free email marketing platforms, free email blast service and free automation tools</h2>
      <p>Looking for free email marketing platforms or a free email blast service for one announcement to people who opted in? Realistic free options in 2026 are HubSpot (2,000 sends a month), Zoho Campaigns (about 6,000 a month), Brevo (300 a day) and small tiers on Mailchimp and MailerLite. These are the best free email automation tools and free email marketing tools for small business, but only for opted-in lists.</p>
      <p>For cold outreach, free barely exists. Marketing senders will suspend you, and dedicated tools start around $37 a month. Start free with Zoho or HubSpot, then upgrade when you hit caps.</p>

      <h2 id="features">Which features matter in email campaign tools?</h2>
      <p>Feature lists blur together, so judge email campaign tools on the few things that move results:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mailbox rotation and warm-up.</strong> Spreading volume across several warmed mailboxes protects your main domain. Cold email tools include this. Marketing platforms don't.</li>
        <li><strong>Unified inbox and reply detection.</strong> Replies are the goal, so you want them in one place, with out-of-office and unsubscribe replies handled automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Sequencing and A/B testing.</strong> Test subject lines and first lines before scaling a campaign to thousands of contacts.</li>
        <li><strong>CRM sync.</strong> Booked meetings should reach your pipeline automatically. HubSpot and Zoho win here, while cold email tools rely on integrations.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing model.</strong> Per contact (Mailchimp, MailerLite), per email (Brevo), per seat (HubSpot, lemlist) or per credit (ManyReach) all behave differently as you grow.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Three mistakes that sink an email campaign</h2>
      <p><strong>Using the wrong category of tool.</strong> Sending cold prospects through a marketing platform is the fastest route to a suspended account. <strong>Skipping warm-up.</strong> A brand-new mailbox that suddenly sends hundreds of emails looks like spam, so ramp up slowly. <strong>Buying the biggest plan first.</strong> Start on a free or entry tier, prove the offer works with a small batch, then pay for scale.</p>

      <h2 id="sec9">How to choose: a four-question shortcut</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Did the recipient opt in?</strong> Yes means a marketing platform. No means a cold email platform.</li>
        <li><strong>Do you already run a CRM?</strong> HubSpot users start with HubSpot. Zoho users start with Zoho Campaigns.</li>
        <li><strong>How many mailboxes will you send from?</strong> More than a few points to Instantly, Smartlead or ManyReach.</li>
        <li><strong>One seat or a team?</strong> HubSpot and lemlist charge per seat. Instantly and Smartlead don't.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="sec10">Email campaign examples: a short cold email that gets replies</h2>
      <div class="pull">Subject: quick question about {{company}}'s onboarding<br><br>Hi {{first_name}}, I noticed {{company}} just launched {{specific_thing}}. We help teams like yours cut onboarding time by automating {{process}}. Worth a 15-minute look? If it's not relevant, tell me and I'll stop.</div>
      <p>Good email campaign design for cold outreach is plain text, under 100 words, with one ask. Run a three-email sequence over about ten days and personalize the first line.</p>

      <h2 id="sec11">Cold email compliance and deliverability checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Authenticate your domain with SPF, DKIM and DMARC.</li>
        <li>Send from secondary domains, never your main one.</li>
        <li>Warm up new mailboxes for a few weeks and keep daily sends per mailbox low.</li>
        <li>Verify your list so bounces stay minimal.</li>
        <li>Follow the <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FTC's CAN-SPAM rules</a>: honest headers, a physical address and a working unsubscribe you honor promptly.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>GDPR in Europe and CASL in Canada are stricter than CAN-SPAM. This isn't legal advice, so check your rules.</p>

